  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\nWe will use a hybrid optimization approach combining low-rank approximation, block coordinate descent, and randomized search to minimize the AC1 upper bound. The core idea is to enforce structure on the sequence (step-function property) while adaptively adjusting block boundaries. This approach offers two key advantages: (1) lower memory usage by maintaining a small number of centroids, and (2) faster convergence by focusing on neighborhood contributions in block updates.\n\nThe central challenge in minimizing AC1 (defined as 2*n*max(convolve(a,a,mode=\"full\"))/sum(a)^2) is balancing normalization stability with autocorrelation reduction. We will use the L2-stable Block coordinate descent (BCD) method that inherently handles continuous bounds. The algorithm proceeds in three nested phases: \n1. Embedding selection (local smoothing)\n2. Energy minimization (direction optimization)\n3. Gradient projection (projection onto feasible set)\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\n- Variables: Centroid embedding vectors f = [f\u2081, f\u2082, ..., f\u2099] \u2208 \u211d\u207f\u208a, norm-constrained by f\u1d40f = \u06f0.5\u0302n (adjust for optimal range)\n- Objective: Minimize g(f) = 2n * max_{\u03c4=-(n-1) to n-1} |(f \u2297 f)[\u03c4]|\n  Where (f \u2297 f)[\u03c4] = \u03a3_{i=0}^{n-1} f[i]f[i+\u03c4] with \u03c4=0 corresponding to the norm\n- Constraints: f_{ij} \u2208 [0, \u06f0] (explicit bounds handled), self-normalization (\u03a3f = c)\n- Steps:\n  f^{k+1} = P_{[0,1000]} [f^k - \u03b7\u2207g(f^k) + \u03bcv^k]  (gradient projection)\n  v^{k+1} = Q(f^{k+1}) (local defining oscillation)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\nUse gradient-based coordinate descent with:\n\nInitialize embedding: \nf\u2080 = uniform_unit_vector(n)  (proj. onto L2-ball)\n# Formulation-dependent structure embedded in the coordinate definitions\n\nConvolution computation:\n\u03b7 = 0.01 + (budget_s-30) seq_length^-0.5\nv^k = (alternate direction)\nconv = full_convolve(f)\n\u03bc = max_ij|conv[i,j]|\n\nBlock-wise coordinate update:\n\nfor k=0 to iters:\n  Find active indices S = {i: r_{ii} > \u03c4 * \u03bc}\n  Sample m = min(50, 0.1*n) blocks uniformly\n  For each center block:\n      Solve auxiliary quadratic program\n      Incorporate second order curvature via Hessian-vector mult\n\nValidation: \nresult = clip_seq(normalize(results_seq)) \nfinal_score = evaluate_sequence(result)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n- Primary variation: Center-based embedding (potentially multiple clusters, but single cluster)\n- Inspecting BCD trajectory for converging directions\n- Initialization set to uniform noise at various amplitudes\n- Learning rate adaptation tracked by curvature eigenvalues\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n- Staged execution: \u03b2\u2080=0.8, \u03b2\u2081=0.5 (curvature reduction)\n- Time allocation: Linear descent (70%), projection warmup (20%), and cluster update (10%)\n- Error checking: Jump in score triggers restart with different m\n- Fallback: Uniform embedding with sum(s)=0.05 if optimization fails\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\nEnsure final sequence is verified by exact AC1 calculation after projection step. Include bound checking throughout (checking self-normalization, bounds). Return closest to optimal solution if stuck.\n\nKey operations (implmenting details):\n1. Full convolution implemented with FFT (n log n complexity) but adaptive for small n via direct convolution\n2. Second-op\uc870\uac74 analytic gradient via difference quotient for the maximum (sparsification)\n3. Projection mapping central coordinates\n4. Dynamically adjust norm during pre-normalization\n\nImplementation strategy: \nUse separate optimization cycles for different dimensions. Start with smallest feasible length (n\u2080=64) then double until budget exceeds. Save best candidate at each phase. Avoid matrix storage unless n>768.\n\nThis approach reduces memory footprint while maintaining mathematical soundness. It handles the tradeoff between smoothness and structure that has historically hampered this class of optimization problems.",
